# What a graph draws: each node's touches and what it does instead, each drawn where it is. `map` hands its closure each
# node of the graph, and the closure hands `Touch::drawn`, by its path, each touch of the node and each of what it does
# instead (`chain` goes on with the elements it is handed). Every element is a part of the graph the route is handed,
# glued into the step as a projection is, so the step takes the graph (#166) and no Touch comes from beyond. The route
# gives back a list of pairs, a type the code does not name, and no Where of its own (#177): the Wheres `Touch::drawn`
# makes go on, through `map` and `collect`, into the pairs the body's own code returns.

route drawing.drawn
step drawn/Touch.drawn
step drawn/Touch.drawn/Where()

line entry > drawn call
line Graph·g > drawn value
line Graph·g > drawn/Touch.drawn/Where() value               # `self.op` of each touch, a part of the graph
line drawn/Touch.drawn/Where() > Where@drawn/Touch.drawn/Where() value
line Where@drawn/Touch.drawn/Where() > drawn/Touch.drawn value
line drawn/Touch.drawn > Where@drawn/Touch.drawn value
line Where@drawn/Touch.drawn > drawn value                    # collected into the pairs the body gives back, by its own code

home Graph·g drawn
home Where@drawn/Touch.drawn/Where() drawn/Touch.drawn
home Where@drawn/Touch.drawn drawn

side entry left
